{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NiWZjPb9xF2hPHVZ5nvt8b","type":"tool_result","content":"# Getting started\n\nThis takes a repository from nothing to a first triaged issue. Run the `ard` commands from a clone of the target repository, or pass `--repo owner/name`.\n\n## 1. Create and install the GitHub App\n\nard acts as a GitHub App, never as a personal token. The quickest way to create one with the right permissions is the manifest flow:\n\n```sh\nard setup --create-gh-app --dry-run   # preview the manifest and URL; creates nothing\nard setup --create-gh-app\n```\n\nard opens a page on a loopback-only local server, you confirm the App on GitHub once, and ard stores the private key in `~/.ard/keys/<slug>.pem` (mode 0600; change it with `--key-file`, overwrite with `--force`). It then prints the install link, waits for you to install the App on the repository, prints the `ARD_GITHUB_*` lines to add to ard's environment, and runs the normal setup checks. The App name defaults to `ard-<owner>-<repo>`; override it with `--app-name`.\n\n### Fallback: create the App by hand\n\nCreate an App you own and give it exactly these repository permissions:\n\n| Permission | Access |\n| --- | --- |\n| Contents | Read and write |\n| Issues | Read and write |\n| Pull requests | Read and write |\n| Checks | Read and write |\n| Commit statuses | Read |\n| Metadata | Read |\n\nDo **not** grant `workflows`. Without it GitHub itself rejects any push that edits `.github/workflows/`. Install the App on the target repository, then note the App ID and installation ID and download the private key (a `.pem` file; keep it mode 0600).\n\nGive `ard serve` the App through its environment: `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\n\n## 2. Let the App push to main\n\nard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`. If `main` has a ruleset, add the App to the ruleset's bypass list. Without that, pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n\n## 3. Audit the bot's commits\n\nSet the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n\n## 4. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init\n```\n\n`ard init` opens a pull request with the starter `.ard/` tree: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. It also writes `.ard/ard-init.lock`, the hash of each starter file it wrote. Review and merge it. Rerunning `ard init` after an upgrade updates only files you have not edited, never touches an edited file (it prints a diff against the starter instead) and never deletes. Then:\n\n```sh\nard labels sync\nard config check\n```\n\n`ard labels sync` creates the `ard:` labels and never deletes one. `ard config check` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\n\n## 5. Store the model credential\n\nSet `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (a subscription token from `claude setup-token`) or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in the environment of `ard serve`. By default the token wins when both are set; `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` set to `oauth` or `apikey` forces one. The value is passed to the agent job and is never logged. If you use a subscription, turn off extra usage in the account so a usage limit is not billed as overage.\n\n## 6. Start serving\n\nTry a dry run first. It logs every side effect without performing it:\n\n```sh\nard serve --dry-run\n```\n\nThen start it for real:\n\n```sh\nard serve\n```\n\n## 7. File and label a first issue\n\nFile an issue describing a small change. With the default `intake.mode: label`, add the `ard:pending` label to admit it. Within one triage interval (15 minutes by default) the lead writes `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` and the issue gains a triage-done label. If `require_approval` is on, add `ard:approved` to let a worker start.\n\nIf nothing happens, see [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).\n# Onboard another repository (#304)\n\nHow to run ard on a second repository, by hand and in order. The model is one\n`ard serve` per repository. See\n[docs/proposal.md: Rollout](../proposal.md#rollout) and\n[Configuration](../proposal.md#configuration).\n\nEach `ard serve` has its own GitHub credentials (a GitHub App installation, or a\nfine-grained PAT after #302), its own state directory and its own limits. ard\ndoes not coordinate across processes. Roles live in the repository's own\n`.ard/`.\n\n## 1. Install the GitHub App\n\nInstall the App on the target repository only. A fine-grained PAT is an\nalternative only once #302 lands; until then use the App.\n\n## 2. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init --repo <owner>/<name>\n```\n\nThis opens an `ard-init` PR. Merge it with **Squash** or a **merge commit**,\nnever a rebase merge (see the `ard init` lane in\n[Guardrails](../proposal.md#guardrails)).\n\n## 3. Sync labels\n\n```sh\nard labels sync\n```\n\n## 4. Branch protection on `main`\n\nProtect `main` with a ruleset and put the App on its bypass list (mode Always).\nThe committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main`; see \"Branch\nprotection and the bot\" in the proposal.\n\n## 5. Copy the bot-commit audit\n\nBy hand, in the target repository (do not edit `.github/` here): copy\n`.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` from this repository, then set the\nrepository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login.\n\n## 6. Set this serve's limits\n\n`max_pods`, `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` in `.ard/config.yaml` apply\nto this `ard serve` alone. When a host or cluster runs more than one, set each\nserve's limits so the **total** fits the host, the cluster and the model\nsubscription.\n\nWorked example: two repositories, A and B, share one host and one subscription.\nThe host runs 4 jobs at once and the budget for the subscription is 60 USD a day.\n\n| Setting | Repo A | Repo B | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `max_pods` | 2 | 2 | 4 (the host's capacity) |\n| `budget_usd_per_day` | 30 | 30 | 60 (the daily budget) |\n| `max_budget_usd` (per job) | 5 | 5 | at most 4 jobs x 5 = 20 in flight |\n\nIf A is the busier repository, give it more of both, but keep each column's sum\nwithin the total.\n\n**Token caps.** ard has no token-cap setting today, and this runbook does not\ninvent one. Divide the subscription's token allowance through `max_pods` and the\ndollar caps above. Each serve records a usage limit on its own, so one serve\nhitting the subscription limit does not stop the other until it hits it too.\n\n## 7. Dry run, soak, go live\n\n```sh\nard serve --dry-run\n```\n\nDry-run keeps its own state in `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/` and writes\nintended effects to `effects.jsonl`. Soak and read the ledger, then start\n`ard serve` without the flag.\n\nIn the cluster, add an entry for the repository to the instances list in the\ndeployment repository (check the key name in that repository's docs). Live agent\njobs in the cluster need #54; until it lands, run the cluster instance in\ndry-run only.\n\n## 8. Optional: a shared roles repository\n\n`roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads\nroles from a shared repository, and the repository's own role of the same name\nwins. Pin a full commit SHA. The shared roles repository is tracked in #59; use\nthis only where it is available. See\n[Configuration](../proposal.md#configuration).\n9:## How it works\n26:## Configuration by convention\n42:## Development\n54:## License\ndocs/site/operations.md:53:Build the new `ard`, stop `ard serve` and start the new binary. Run `ard config check` first, because a new release may reject a key the old one accepted. Review the release for changes to the starter roles; `ard init` proposes updates as a pull request: it updates starter files you have not edited (judged by `.ard/ard-init.lock`), leaves edited files alone and prints their diff, and never deletes.\ndocs/site/troubleshooting.md:26:- Check `ard config check` and that `paused` is not true.\ndocs/site/troubleshooting.md:33:`ard config check` names the file and key. Common causes are an unknown key, a duration written in days, or an `intake.label` that is one of ard's own labels.\ndocs/site/troubleshooting.md:37:Run `ard labels sync`. It creates and updates labels and reports stale ones, but never deletes them.\ndocs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md:70:   `ard config check --repo albertcbrown/ard-scratch --ref origin/ard-init`\ndocs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md:137:   `./bin/ard labels sync --repo albertcbrown/ard`.\ndocs/site/getting-started.md:50:ard labels sync\ndocs/site/getting-started.md:51:ard config check\ndocs/site/getting-started.md:54:`ard labels sync` creates the `ard:` labels and never deletes one. `ard config check` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\ndocs/site/index.md:18:GitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. Most follow `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]`, for example `ard:triage:lead:requested`. Run `ard labels sync` to create them. The ones you will touch:\ndocs/site/configuration.md:3:ard is configured by convention. A role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. Anything unset takes the default below. Check your file with `ard config check`; `ard serve` reads it from `origin/main`, so a change takes effect once it is merged. The full design is in the [proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\ncmd_config.go\ncmd_init.go\ncmd_job.go\ncmd_labels.go\ncmd_rebuild.go\ncmd_serve.go\ncmd_setup.go\ncmd_version.go","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"s