{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChPcNrNB3HvJoTLfyQAT8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now rewrite getting-started sections 2–7."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":466,"cache_read_input_tokens":19908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":466},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d3d22016-c02a-4824-9a50-9b114b5bbbcd","uuid":"75824e79-b0e0-4562-b502-d137e0aacca3","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:01:20.602Z","request_id":"req_011ChPcNrEEfwgU97NfZVbD1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChPcNrNB3HvJoTLfyQAT8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SmwJtSGpJoKdoZGKLULPEY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='docs/site/getting-started.md'\ns=open(p).read()\na=s.index('## 2. Let the App push')\nb=s.index('## 5. Store the model')\nc=s.index('## 6. Start serving')\nnew='''## 2. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init\n```\n\n`ard init` writes the starter `.ard/` tree into your local clone: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. It also writes `.ard/ard-init.lock`, the hash of each starter file it wrote. Review the files, commit them on a branch and merge them by pull request. Merge with **Squash** or a **merge commit**, never fast-forward or rebase. To have ard open the pull request for you, run `ard init --pr` instead; it opens the `ard-init` PR.\n\nNothing takes effect until the tree is merged, because `ard serve` reads its configuration from `origin/main`. Rerunning `ard init` after an upgrade updates only files you have not edited, never touches an edited file (it prints a diff against the starter instead) and never deletes.\n\n## 3. Run setup\n\n```sh\nard setup\n```\n\n`ard setup` creates the `ard:` labels (it never deletes one) and checks the App's permissions, the ruleset on `main` and the App's bypass, the App permissions (no `workflows` or administration) and the merge settings. It prints one row per check: `permissions`, `labels`, `ruleset`, `app-permissions` and `merge-settings`, each with the status ok, changed, drift or manual.\n\nTo report without changing anything, run `ard setup --check`. It exits with code 2 when anything has drifted.\n\n## 4. Fix any manual rows\n\nA row that setup cannot fix or read for itself prints as `manual: <name>: <fix>`. The text after the name is the exact fix, so do what it says. The usual ones:\n\n- `ruleset`: if `main` has a ruleset, it must require a pull request and let only the ard App bypass it. ard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`, and without the bypass those pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n- `app-permissions`: remove any permission the row names from the App.\n- `merge-settings`: turn off rebase merging, as the row says.\n\nAlso audit the bot's commits. Set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n\nRerun `ard setup` until no row is drift or manual. `ard config check` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\n\n'''\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:c].replace('## 5.','## 5.')+s[c:]\ns=s.replace('## 6. Start serving\\n\\nTry a dry run first.','## 6. Dry run, then go live\\n\\nTry a dry run first.')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\n\np='docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md'\ns=open(p).read()\na=s.index('## 1. Install'); b=s.index('## 6. Set')\nnew='''## 1. Install the GitHub App\n\nInstall the App on the target repository only. A fine-grained PAT is an\nalternative only once #302 lands; until then use the App.\n\n## 2. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init --repo <owner>/<name>\n```\n\nThis writes the starter `.ard/` tree locally. Commit it on a branch and merge\nit with **Squash** or a **merge commit**, never fast-forward or rebase (see the\n`ard init` lane in [Guardrails](../proposal.md#guardrails)). Nothing takes\neffect until it is merged, because `ard serve` reads `origin/main`. Use\n`ard init --pr` to have ard open the `ard-init` PR.\n\n## 3. Run setup\n\n```sh\nard setup --repo <owner>/<name>\n```\n\nThis creates the labels and checks permissions, the ruleset and App bypass, App\npermissions and merge settings. `ard setup --check` only reports and exits 2 on\ndrift.\n\n## 4. Fix the manual rows\n\nEach `manual: <name>: <fix>` row carries the exact fix. Typical ones:\n\n- `ruleset`: protect `main` with a ruleset and put the App on its bypass list\n  (mode Always). The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to\n  `main`; see \"Branch protection and the bot\" in the proposal.\n- `merge-settings`: turn off rebase merging.\n- Bot-commit audit, by hand in the target repository (do not edit `.github/`\n  here): copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` from this repository, then set\n  the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login.\n\nRerun `ard setup` until it is green.\n\n## 5. Set this serve's limits\n'''\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:].replace('## 6. Set this serve\\'s limits\\n','',1).lstrip('\\n')\ns=s.replace('## 7. Dry run, soak','## 6. Dry run, soak')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\n\np='README.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('## Development','''## Getting started\n\nInstall the App, run `ard init`, commit and merge the tree, run `ard setup`, fix any manual rows it reports, try `ard serve --dry-run`, then go live. See [Getting started](docs/site/getting-started.md).\n\n## Development''',1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\n\np='docs/site/troubleshooting.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('Run `ard labels sync`.','Run `ard setup`.')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='docs/site/index.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('Run `ard labels sync` to create them','Run `ard setup` to create them')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\nsed -n 55,75p docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md; go test ./internal/docscheck/... 2>&1 | tail -5; git status --porcelain"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":466,"cache_read_input_tokens":19908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":466},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d3d22016-c02a-4824-9a50-9b114b5bbbcd","uuid":"ad12c7d0-e447-46d1-80c1-3add27e382f9","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:01:32.320Z","request_id":"req_011ChPcNrEEfwgU97NfZVbD1"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SmwJtSGpJoKdoZGKLULPEY","type":"tool_result","content":"`max_pods`, `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` in `.ard/config.yaml` apply\nto this `ard serve` alone. When a host or cluster runs more than one, set each\nserve's limits so the **total** fits the host, the cluster and the model\nsubscription.\n\nWorked example: two repositories, A and B, share one host and one subscription.\nThe host runs 4 jobs at once and the budget for the subscription is 60 USD a day.\n\n| Setting | Repo A | Repo B | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `max_pods` | 2 | 2 | 4 (the host's capacity) |\n| `budget_usd_per_day` | 30 | 30 | 60 (the daily budget) |\n| `max_budget_usd` (per job) | 5 | 5 | at most 4 jobs x 5 = 20 in flight |\n\nIf A is the busier repository, give it more of both, but keep each column's sum\nwithin the total.\n\n**Token caps.** ard has no token-cap setting today, and this runbook does not\ninvent one. Divide the subscription's token allowance through `max_pods` and the\ndollar caps above. Each serve records a usage limit on its own, so one serve\nhitting the subscription limit does not stop the other until it hits it too.\ngo: downloading github.com/spf13/cobra v1.10.2\ngo: downloading github.com/spf13/pflag v1.0.9\ngo: downloading github.com/bmatcuk/doublestar/v4 v4.10.4\ngo: downloading gopkg.in/yaml.v3 v3.0.1\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/docscheck\t0.358s\n M README.md\n M docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\n M docs/site/getting-started.md\n M docs/site/index.md\n M docs/site/troubleshooting.md\n?? .ard-input/","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d3d22016-c02a-4824-9a50-9b114b5bbbcd","uuid":"8b85eb6a-a9fb-497a-93a9-ab5a12110996","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:01:35.924Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"`max_pods`, `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` in `.ard/config.yaml` apply\nto this `ard serve` alone. When a host or cluster runs more than one, set each\nserve's limits so the **total** fits the host, the cluster and the model\nsubscription.\n\nWorked example: two repositories, A and B, share one host and one subscription.\nThe host runs 4 jobs at once and the budget for the subscription is 60 USD a day.\n\n| Setting | Repo A | Repo B | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `max_pods` | 2 | 2 | 4 (the host's capacity) |\n| `budget_usd_per_day` | 30 | 30 | 60 (the daily budget) |\n| `max_budget_usd` (per job) | 5 | 5 | at most 4 jobs x 5 = 20 in flight |\n\nIf A is the busier repository, give it more of both, but keep each column's sum\nwithin the total.\n\n**Token caps.** ard has no token-cap setting today, and this runbook does not\ninvent one. Divide the subscription's token allowance through `max_pods` and the\ndollar caps above. Each serve records a usage limit on its own, so one serve\nhitting the subscription limit does not stop the other until it hits it too.\ngo: downloading github.com/spf13/cobra v1.10.2\ngo: downloading github.com/spf13/pflag v1.0.9\ngo: downloading github.com/bmatcuk/doublestar/v4 v4.10.4\ngo: downloading gopkg.in/yaml.v3 v3.0.1\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/docscheck\t0.358s\n M README.md\n M docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\n M docs/site/getting-started.md\n M docs/site/index.md\n M docs/site/troubleshooting.md\n?? .ard-input/","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SmwJtSGpJoKdoZGKLULPEY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
